def rectangles_overlap(r1, r2):
    pairs = ((r1[0], r2[0]), (r1[1], r2[1]), (r1[2], r2[2]), (r1[3], r2[3]))
    for lo_a, lo_b in pairs:
        if lo_a >= lo_b:
            return False
    return True
```

The four pairs tested in the function are each of a lower coordinate from one rectangle against the corresponding lower coordinate from the other rectangle, and likewise for pairs of rightsides. The rectangles overlap in area only when both rectangles extend beyond each other on the x-axis and on the y-axis; inequality in one or both directions means they are disjoint or border without enclosing a region. The first rectangle being smaller than the second says nothing about whether they overlap, so all four pairs have to be checked.

For the examples, (0, 0, 2, 2) against (1, 1, 3, 3) is accepted because both 1 < 2 and 2 < 3 hold, which is correct even though each touches the other at a side, the spec requires interior overlap. The second assert fails only because there is no loop, so it is simply answered False, which is right for two rectangles that are far apart. For (0, 0, 4, 4) and (1, 1, 2, 2), the inner rectangle sits entirely inside the outer one, and every pair satisfies the strict opposite ordering. The code never looks at any of the three particular rectangles in the examples.

The implementation is equivalent to the interior-overlap formula for rectangles aligned along a lattice grid: those that do not lie strictly to one side of each other on both axes.

